{
 "id": "DW-02",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-dw-02-eee071cc36",
 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 6,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "My Samsung dishwasher DW60M6050FS keeps showing 4C and beeping, won't do anything"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "4C on your Samsung means: Water supply issue - little/no fresh water enters (supply valve/frozen/clogged inlet). From what you have described, the most likely cause is water supply / isolation tap / inlet hose / inlet filter (check first). Can you tell me whether no or low water?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "No water at all in the tub as far as I can tell, no filling noise either. It started after a plumber fitted a new kitchen tap yesterday."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is appliance isolation tap / supply. Open the door, make sure nothing is caught in the seal, then close it firmly until it clicks and start a programme. Does the door click locked now?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I'm not sure the door is the problem. I closed it firmly and it clicked, but it still beeps and shows 4C shortly after starting. Is there anything to check on the water supply side?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you should check that the hot and cold water taps behind the machine are fully open. Also ensure the inlet hoses are not kinked or blocked by debris from the recent plumbing work. Check the tap the fill hose connects to is turned fully on, and that the hose behind the machine isn't kinked or squashed against the wall. Was the tap fully on and the hose clear, did you find it off or kinked (and sort it), or is the hose itself damaged?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The hose has no kinks. The valve under the sink has a screwdriver slot and it's at 90 degrees, across the pipe rather than along it. Does that mean it's off?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Before you start, switch it off and unplug it. Yes, that position indicates the water supply is turned off. You need to turn the handle so it aligns parallel with the pipe to restore flow. Where the fill hose screws onto the back of the machine there's a small mesh filter that can block with grit or scale. Unscrew the hose at the machine end and look into the inlet. Was the mesh blocked (and is it clean now), or was it already clean?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Where the fill-hose mesh filter is\" — A small mesh filter sits inside the fill-hose connector at the tap/valve end and catches grit. Turn the tap off, unscrew the hose and rinse the mesh. Typical layout — your machine may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I haven't unscrewed the hose yet. I'll turn the valve so the slot runs along the pipe first and see if it fills, then check the mesh if it still doesn't."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Now turn the tap back on and start a programme. Does it fill normally now, or is it still not taking water?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Turned the valve so the slot runs along the pipe and started a programme. It's filling now and running normally, no 4C. Thanks!"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Good news, that has sorted it. If it happens again, start with the same check. Is there anything else I can help with?"
  }
 ],
